<Base
  title="Accessibility"
  description="Accessibility statement for DaoismHub — the standard the site aims at, what is currently supported, known limitations and how to report a barrier."
>
  <h1>Accessibility</h1>
  <p class="lede">Version 1.0 &middot; Last updated: October 21, 2026 &middot; Applies to daoismhub.com and data.daoismhub.com</p>

  <h2>Our commitment</h2>
  <p>
    DaoismHub is a reading site: its value is text, and text should be readable by everyone.
    We aim to meet the <strong>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.2 at Level AA</strong>
    for the pages of both sites. This page describes what the site currently does, where we
    know it falls short, and how to tell us about a barrier we have missed.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not claim certification or third-party audit. The statement below is an honest
    description of the site's current state and its intent, written and maintained by the
    editor rather than by an accessibility vendor.
  </p>

  <h2>What the site does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Semantic structure.</strong> Pages use real headings in a logical order, real
      lists, real tables where tabular data is presented, and a single main heading per page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Landmarks and navigation.</strong> The layout uses navigation, main and content
      landmarks, and a skip link so keyboard users can bypass the navigation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keyboard operation.</strong> Links, navigation and interactive controls can be
      reached and operated with a keyboard, and focus is visible when you tab through a page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Text and contrast.</strong> Body text is set at a readable size with a line
      length intended for sustained reading, and the palette is designed so that text meets
      the AA contrast ratio against its background. Information is not conveyed by colour
      alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Resizing and reflow.</strong> Text can be enlarged with browser zoom, and
      content reflows rather than requiring horizontal scrolling at narrow viewports.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Language.</strong> The page language is declared in the document itself, and key
      passages in Chinese carry a language attribute so that screen readers switch pronunciation
      for those passages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Motion.</strong> The site does not autoplay video or audio and does not use
      animated content that flashes. The site also honours the operating system's
      reduced-motion setting, suppressing non-essential motion for visitors who enable it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No time limits, no CAPTCHAs, no interstitials.</strong> Pages do not expire, do
      not ask you to prove you are human to read, and do not interrupt reading with pop-ups.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Search without tracking.</strong> The Data Registry's search runs in the browser
      and works without an account.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>
  <p>We are aware of the following, and would rather name them than hide them:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Classical Chinese and transliterations.</strong> Passages in literary Chinese and
      diacritic-bearing romanisations (for example <em>dàodé jīng</em>) can be pronounced
      imperfectly by screen readers, depending on the voice and language pack installed. The
      text and its translation are always given together to reduce reliance on pronunciation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Diagrams and tables in the data pages.</strong> Some structured records are
      presented as tables; on very small screens these may require scrolling within the table
      region. We are working to improve reflow for these.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PDF and external sources.</strong> Some linked scholarly sources are PDFs or
      third-party pages whose accessibility we do not control.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Older pages.</strong> Pages written earlier in the project may not yet have been
      re-checked against the current heading and landmark patterns.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Assistive technology</h2>
  <p>
    The site is built as static HTML with no client-side framework required to read the text,
    which is the most compatible arrangement for assistive technology. It should work with
    current versions of screen readers (NVDA, JAWS, VoiceOver, TalkBack), with browser zoom, and
    with operating-system high-contrast and reduced-motion settings. We test by reading the
    site with a keyboard and a screen reader; we do not have the resources to test every
    browser and assistive-technology combination.
  </p>

  <h2>How to report a barrier</h2>
  <p>
    If something on either site is difficult or impossible to use — a control you cannot reach,
    text you cannot read, a heading that makes no sense aloud, a table that breaks when
    enlarged — please email <strong>martinwang2024@gmail.com</strong> with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>the page URL;</li>
    <li>what you were trying to do;</li>
    <li>what happened instead;</li>
    <li>the browser, device and assistive technology you were using, if you are willing to share them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We aim to acknowledge accessibility reports within seven days and to fix confirmed barriers
    as soon as practicable. Fixes are made to the page itself, not only to this statement.
  </p>

  <h2>Standards and references</h2>
  <p>
    The target standard is WCAG 2.2 Level AA, published by the World Wide Web Consortium
    (W3C). Where this statement and the standard differ, the standard governs and the
    statement is out of date — tell us and we will correct it.
  </p>

